	"nodes": [
		{
			"id": "header",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "## CPTSD\n\nCondition hub: the ICD-11 complex PTSD construct, borrowed mechanisms with their limits, assessment, and the treatment sequencing debate.\n\n[[CPTSD Map|Open the domain map]] - snapshot of 2026-09-27; the map's *In this folder* section is the live view.",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 0,
			"width": 1800,
			"height": 170,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[CPTSD and disturbances in self-organization]]**\n*condition* - 8 sources\n\nThe ICD-11 complex PTSD construct, its DSO clusters, the exposure requirement and the instrument used to measure it.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "t0_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Post-traumatic stress disorder]]**\n*condition* - 13 sources\n\nThe PTSD diagnosis itself: DSM and ICD-11 criteria, prevalence and course, first-line treatment classes, and how the diagnosis relates to stress-response mechanisms and to ICD-11 complex PTSD.",
			"x": 30,
			"y": 622,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "g0",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Constructs",
			"x": 0,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 578
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Trauma and stress responses]]**\n*process* - 8 sources\n\nAppraisal, acute versus chronic stress physiology, the immune route, and the step from traumatic exposure to a symptom-based diagnosis.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Fear learning and extinction]]**\n*mechanism* - 7 sources\n\nThe laboratory model underlying exposure treatment, its mechanisms and the reasons relapse occurs.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 622,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Memory and trauma]]**\n*framework* - 6 sources\n\nWhat is established about memory for traumatic events, and why both popular exaggeration and dismissal are wrong.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 860,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_3",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[PTSD biology and CPTSD evidence limits]]**\n*framework* - 12 sources\n\nWhat PTSD biology covers, why it does not transfer to complex PTSD, and the status of the dissociative subtype as the best-supported subdivision.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 1098,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_4",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Trauma and PTSD in autistic and ADHD people]]**\n*framework* - 10 sources\n\nHow often trauma exposure and PTSD are recorded in autistic and ADHD people, why assessment is harder, and how far trauma treatments have been adapted and tested.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 1384,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "t1_5",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Adverse childhood experiences]]**\n*framework* - 6 sources\n\nThe ACE framework of counted childhood adversities, its dose-response associations with later health, how much of the association is causal, and the limits of retrospective counts and of ACE scores for individuals.",
			"x": 490,
			"y": 1670,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 250,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "g1",
			"type": "group",
			"label": "Mechanisms and models",
			"x": 460,
			"y": 310,
			"width": 420,
			"height": 1650
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_0",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Phase-based trauma therapy evidence]]**\n*framework* - 4 sources\n\nThe stabilisation-first rationale, the comparative evidence, and what the sequencing debate is actually about.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 360,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 178,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_1",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Psychological interventions for complex trauma]]**\n*framework* - 5 sources\n\nWhat pooled psychotherapy effects in complex trauma show, which moderators weaken them, and why trial categories are not diagnoses.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 598,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 226,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
		{
			"id": "t2_2",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**[[Recovery function and social context]]**\n*framework* - 6 sources\n\nWhat recovery means in complex trauma work, how function is measured, and why social conditions belong in the outcome picture.",
			"x": 950,
			"y": 884,
			"width": 360,
			"height": 202,
			"color": "#FF6B5B"
		},
